Recommended Free I2V (Image-to-Video) Motion Engines
Based on the research, these are the free/local options that pair with FLUX:

1. WanVideo 2.1 (via ComfyUI) — Best Overall for Low VRAM

The Flux.1 + WanVideo workflow is explicitly designed for low VRAM (≤8GB) and integrates FLUX image generation directly with WanVideo I2V conversion. The workflow: Flux.1 generates a high-quality still from a text prompt → WanVideo animates it into a short video → output MP4.

Required models:

flux1-dev.sft (FLUX image generation)

wan2.1_i2v_480p_14B_bf16_Comfy-Org.safetensors (motion)

WanVideo T5 encoder, CLIP, and VAE

2. FLUX 3 Video I2V (via BFL API) — For Keyframe-Controlled Loops

Black Forest Labs' own FLUX 3 Video API supports Image-to-Video with three modes:

Mode	Input	Best For Looping
Startframe	1 image	Animating forward from a still
Start + End	2 images	Perfect for loops — use the same image as both start and end, prompt for cyclical motion (steam, rain, orbit, ambient drift)
Keyframes	3+ images	Choreographing through specific poses/compositions
For a 3–5 second seamless loop: feed the same image as both first and last frame, set duration to 3–5s, and prompt for cyclical motion (e.g., "steam rising and dissipating, camera slowly orbiting, ambient light pulsing").

The bfl-api Node.js wrapper gives you CLI access: bfl --flux-3-video --video-mode i2v --prompt "..." --duration 5.

3. dgenerate (CLI + Library)

Scriptable command-line tool for generating images and animation sequences using stable diffusion techniques, with a GUI scripting environment. Good if you want programmatic batch animation generation.

The Seamless Loop Technique
For a 3–5 second looping animation (as opposed to a one-shot clip):

Generate the still with FLUX (text-to-image or img2img).

Use Start + End frame mode with the same image for both — this forces the model to return to the starting composition.

Prompt for cyclical motion: "seamless loop, returning to the starting composition, cyclical ambient motion, no cuts".

Duration: set to 3–5 seconds. FLUX 3 video supports up to 20s, but 3–5s is the sweet spot for social loops.

Post-process with ffmpeg: use the loopable-video-segmenter already in your repo (lib/loopable-video-segmenter/) to beat-align and mirror-loop the output if needed.

